WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:06.370
When it comes to exporting and importing your animation assets and your characters,

2
00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:11.070
it's important to be aware of some of the settings that you need to get accurate results.

3
00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:14.570
So I'm going to go over some of the settings for a few different softwares.

4
00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:18.370
We'll look at Maya, we'll look at 3ds Max and Blender,

5
00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:24.190
as these are some of the most popular external animation softwares that people tend to use.

6
00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:28.989
If you're exporting from Maya, make sure that you select all of the joints in your skeleton,

7
00:00:28.989 --> 00:00:30.989
as well as the skinned mesh,

8
00:00:30.989 --> 00:00:32.990
and choose File, Export.

9
00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:36.619
Change the export type to FBX,

10
00:00:36.619 --> 00:00:39.320
and make sure that you tick the animation checkbox,

11
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.079
so that the animation comes out with the export.

12
00:00:43.079 --> 00:00:47.880
Make sure at the bottom of the window here you have deformed models, skins and blend shapes ticked

13
00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.579
if they're relevant for your character.

14
00:00:49.579 --> 00:00:53.670
One thing to note is if you want the smoothing information for your character,

15
00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:58.170
disable Smooth Mesh, and instead tick the Smoothing Groups option.

16
00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:02.170
Now this allows Unreal to calculate the smoothing of the mesh more accurately,

17
00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:05.170
and gives you more control over the smoothing on import.

18
00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:09.879
Exporting from 3ds Max is very, very similar to Maya,

19
00:01:09.879 --> 00:01:14.879
so just make sure that you have all of your assets selected that you want to export.

20
00:01:14.879 --> 00:01:17.879
In the export options, keep animation ticked,

21
00:01:17.879 --> 00:01:22.379
and make sure deformations, skins and morph targets are also all ticked.

22
00:01:22.379 --> 00:01:26.480
If you're using Blender to animate with,

23
00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:33.480
Epic have actually created a plugin that allows you to send your animation straight from Blender to the engine,

24
00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.980
and this can also include your rig from Blender as well.

25
00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:42.480
Now Epic has a YouTube playlist that goes over how to install this and how to use the plugin,

26
00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:48.750
which should speed up your workflow when you're sending stuff from Blender over to the engine.

27
00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:51.750
If you're not using that Unreal plugin,

28
00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:55.250
what you can do is just use the default exporter within Blender.

29
00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:57.750
So in the FBX export settings,

30
00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:02.250
set the forward axis to X forward to match Unreal's forward axis,

31
00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:05.250
and make sure that you disable add leaf bones.

32
00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:10.039
Uncheck bake animation, and then smoothing groups aren't a concept in Blender,

33
00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:13.039
so apply any smoothing modifiers before you export.

34
00:02:13.039 --> 00:02:17.039
So your auto smooth, your edge split, anything like that you need to apply,

35
00:02:17.039 --> 00:02:20.039
get that done before you're doing your export.

36
00:02:20.039 --> 00:02:25.650
Something to keep in mind for anything you're exporting from Blender is the default units.

37
00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:28.650
So in Blender, the default is one meter per unit.

38
00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.650
However, in Unreal, it's one centimeter per unit.

39
00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:34.150
So whether you convert that on import,

40
00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:37.650
or whether you make a change to your Blender settings,

41
00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:41.650
it's just worth keeping that in mind and knowing that there's a difference there.

42
00:02:42.349 --> 00:02:46.849
Now that we know how to correctly export animations from these external softwares,

43
00:02:46.849 --> 00:02:49.349
let's take a look and see how we can do that in engine,

44
00:02:49.349 --> 00:02:55.349
as well as importing into the engine, both your first character, as well as new animations.

45
00:02:55.349 --> 00:02:59.789
So exporting from engine is quite straightforward, actually.

46
00:02:59.789 --> 00:03:03.289
You just find the animation that you want to export in your content browser,

47
00:03:03.289 --> 00:03:08.289
right click, go to asset actions, and then export.

48
00:03:08.289 --> 00:03:11.479
It'll ask you where you want to save it, so we'll just put it on the desktop for now,

49
00:03:11.479 --> 00:03:13.479
and we'll keep the name as it is,

50
00:03:13.479 --> 00:03:16.479
and then you'll get the FBX export options window come up,

51
00:03:16.479 --> 00:03:19.060
which I'll just make a little bit bigger.

52
00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:24.060
Now, a lot of these options you probably don't need to have ticked

53
00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:27.120
when you're just exporting an animation.

54
00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.719
So if you untick vertex color and level of detail, as well as collision,

55
00:03:31.719 --> 00:03:35.719
something you may or may not want to tick is export preview mesh.

56
00:03:35.719 --> 00:03:39.719
If you just want the animation information and the joints,

57
00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:41.719
then you can uncheck this.

58
00:03:41.719 --> 00:03:44.219
But if you want to see the character's mesh as well,

59
00:03:44.219 --> 00:03:46.219
then you should keep that checked.

60
00:03:46.219 --> 00:03:50.419
And then all you need to do is press export.

61
00:03:50.419 --> 00:03:53.919
One thing to note that any meshes that you do export from the engine

62
00:03:53.919 --> 00:03:55.919
will come out triangulated.

63
00:03:55.919 --> 00:03:58.419
And that's just because when you import the mesh into the engine,

64
00:03:58.419 --> 00:04:04.479
Unreal automatically triangulates it just for hardware optimization.

65
00:04:04.479 --> 00:04:06.979
Before you can bring any animations into the engine,

66
00:04:06.979 --> 00:04:09.979
the first thing that you need to do is bring in your character.

67
00:04:09.979 --> 00:04:12.479
And that character is going to include the skeleton

68
00:04:12.479 --> 00:04:15.479
that the animations will play on and the skeletal mesh,

69
00:04:15.479 --> 00:04:19.209
which is essentially the mesh of the character.

70
00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:21.529
So let's go ahead and do that now.

71
00:04:21.529 --> 00:04:25.439
So inside your content browser, if you just click import,

72
00:04:25.439 --> 00:04:29.019
find your character, hit open,

73
00:04:29.019 --> 00:04:32.019
and because we know we want to bring a skeletal mesh in,

74
00:04:32.019 --> 00:04:35.019
there's a skeletal mesh filter option just here.

75
00:04:35.019 --> 00:04:40.240
And this will eliminate all the options that are unnecessary

76
00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:43.740
for this type of import, this type of asset that we're bringing in.

77
00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:47.129
So leaving everything as it is at default,

78
00:04:47.129 --> 00:04:50.120
if I just press import,

79
00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:52.620
you'll see that our characters come in here, our skeleton,

80
00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:55.129
as well as the skeletal mesh.

81
00:04:55.129 --> 00:04:57.629
Now that we have our character in the engine,

82
00:04:57.629 --> 00:04:59.629
we can actually start to bring an animation in.

83
00:04:59.629 --> 00:05:02.129
So this is a similar process.

84
00:05:02.129 --> 00:05:06.230
Find wherever it is you want to store your animation,

85
00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:07.730
and just press import.

86
00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:10.420
We'll use the one that we exported earlier,

87
00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:12.839
and we'll just press open.

88
00:05:12.839 --> 00:05:15.839
Now, because this is an animation, again, we can use the filters here,

89
00:05:15.839 --> 00:05:18.370
so we'll just check animations there.

90
00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:22.370
And automatically, it's chosen my character skeleton

91
00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:24.870
as the skeleton that we want to use.

92
00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:28.370
If it doesn't do that, then you should choose this dropdown

93
00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:30.870
and select your skeleton from the list

94
00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:34.370
because we don't want to bring in any of the skeleton

95
00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:36.870
or the mesh information from this file.

96
00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:39.870
We just want to bring in the animation information.

97
00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:44.329
So if we tick import animations,

98
00:05:44.329 --> 00:05:48.290
and just scroll down a little

99
00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:50.290
because that threw us a little higher up.

100
00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:51.480
There we go.

101
00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:52.480
Oh, no, sorry.

102
00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:55.019
There we go, import animations.

103
00:05:55.019 --> 00:05:57.519
Make sure import bone tracks is ticked.

104
00:05:57.519 --> 00:06:01.089
And under the animation length here,

105
00:06:01.089 --> 00:06:04.389
you've got three different options.

106
00:06:04.389 --> 00:06:06.389
So more often than not,

107
00:06:06.389 --> 00:06:12.389
you'll export from frame 0 to frame whatever, call it 100,

108
00:06:12.389 --> 00:06:16.459
essentially from the first to the last frame of your animation.

109
00:06:16.459 --> 00:06:17.959
And when that's the case,

110
00:06:17.959 --> 00:06:20.959
you just keep this as default set to source timeline,

111
00:06:20.959 --> 00:06:24.180
and that will automatically bring in the entire animation.

112
00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:27.680
And you can select also animated time,

113
00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:31.939
and this imports the range of frames that have animation on them.

114
00:06:31.939 --> 00:06:34.439
And alternatively, you could set a range

115
00:06:34.439 --> 00:06:38.439
and then choose the minimum frame and the maximum frame

116
00:06:38.439 --> 00:06:42.600
of that range that you want to bring in.

117
00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:48.629
By default, the sample rate or the frame rate is 30 FPS.

118
00:06:48.629 --> 00:06:50.129
If yours is different from that,

119
00:06:50.129 --> 00:06:52.129
you can use a custom sample rate,

120
00:06:52.129 --> 00:06:54.709
and you just enter that here,

121
00:06:54.709 --> 00:06:58.629
and then you just press import.

122
00:06:58.629 --> 00:07:01.779
And here we can see our animation

123
00:07:01.779 --> 00:07:05.000
laying on our character.

124
00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:09.170
So it really is quite straightforward.

125
00:07:09.170 --> 00:07:11.170
So now that we've imported our characters

126
00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:14.170
and we've imported our animations into the engine,

127
00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:17.170
what if you make a change to that character or to that animation

128
00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:19.860
and you want to reimport it and bring it back in?

129
00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:21.860
Well, it's actually quite straightforward.

130
00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:23.860
So with animations,

131
00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:28.540
if you right-click them and choose reimport,

132
00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:33.040
as long as you've overwritten that file inside File Explorer,

133
00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:35.040
it will just bring the same file back in

134
00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:38.040
and any edits that you've made and you've overwritten with,

135
00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:40.160
it will reimport that information.

136
00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:42.660
If you wanted to bring in a new file,

137
00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:45.360
or you saved the file somewhere else,

138
00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:49.480
you could reimport with new file.

139
00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:51.480
And this will bring you to your File Explorer,

140
00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:53.480
and you can find the file that you want,

141
00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:57.560
press open, and that'll bring that back in.

142
00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:02.660
The options also exist inside the animation asset itself.

143
00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:05.459
So if we were to open that up,

144
00:08:05.459 --> 00:08:06.959
on the top bar here,

145
00:08:06.959 --> 00:08:09.750
you can see that you can reimport the animation,

146
00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:11.250
and that will do the same thing

147
00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:14.750
It'll find the existing file inside your File Explorer,

148
00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:17.250
and as long as you've overwritten it with your new export,

149
00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:20.240
it'll just bring that straight back in.

150
00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:24.240
And if you want to change any of the reimport options,

151
00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:26.740
you can choose reimport animation with dialogue,

152
00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:30.240
and that brings up your import options.

153
00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:32.740
And anything that you wanted to change in here, you could do.

154
00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:35.240
If you wanted to assign it to a different skeleton,

155
00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:37.740
change the frame rate, anything like that,

156
00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:42.809
you could then change those options and press import.

157
00:08:42.809 --> 00:08:44.809
If you want to reimport the character itself,

158
00:08:44.809 --> 00:08:47.809
you could do that in a similar way to the animation.

159
00:08:47.809 --> 00:08:49.809
All you have to do is right-click the character,

160
00:08:49.809 --> 00:08:52.309
reimport or reimport with new file,

161
00:08:52.309 --> 00:08:54.309
and that'll give you the same options,

162
00:08:54.309 --> 00:08:57.659
or that will reimport in the same way that the animation did.

163
00:08:57.659 --> 00:08:59.659
Or you could open the asset,

164
00:08:59.659 --> 00:09:02.659
and again, you have a reimport mesh option at the top here,

165
00:09:02.659 --> 00:09:04.659
but you also have these three dots,

166
00:09:04.659 --> 00:09:06.659
so you have some extra options.

167
00:09:06.659 --> 00:09:09.659
So you could do the same two options here,

168
00:09:09.659 --> 00:09:12.159
reimport mesh or reimport with new file,

169
00:09:12.159 --> 00:09:14.659
or you could bring in the mesh and the levels of detail,

170
00:09:14.659 --> 00:09:16.659
so the LODs.

171
00:09:16.659 --> 00:09:19.159
Same thing with the new file.

172
00:09:19.159 --> 00:09:21.190
And if there's just specific things like the geometry

173
00:09:21.190 --> 00:09:23.190
or the skin weights that you want to bring in,

174
00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:24.690
you could do that too.

175
00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:27.450
And the same options exist over here

176
00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:31.450
with the addition of the import dialogue option box.

177
00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:33.960
So if you were to select one of those,

178
00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:35.960
you'd get this option box to come up again,

179
00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:42.259
and you could change any of these settings that were relevant.

180
00:09:42.259 --> 00:09:44.759
Another way to add animations to your project

181
00:09:44.759 --> 00:09:48.259
would be to purchase them from the Unreal Fabstore.

182
00:09:48.259 --> 00:09:52.740
So once you've purchased an animation pack from the Fabstore,

183
00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:55.740
you would then find it in your Unreal Engine library,

184
00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:58.740
and it's as simple as clicking Add to Project

185
00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:02.159
and choosing the project that you want to add it to.

186
00:10:02.159 --> 00:10:04.159
Now, what you'll probably find

187
00:10:04.159 --> 00:10:06.159
is that any animations that you download

188
00:10:06.159 --> 00:10:09.159
will very likely be for a different skeleton,

189
00:10:09.159 --> 00:10:11.159
meaning they'll come in with its own skeleton

190
00:10:11.159 --> 00:10:13.190
and its own skeletal mesh.

191
00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:14.690
And what you'll need to do there

192
00:10:14.690 --> 00:10:16.690
is use Unreal Engine's retargeting tool,

193
00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:18.690
which is very simple, very robust.

194
00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:21.190
If you right-click, choose Retarget,

195
00:10:21.190 --> 00:10:24.799
and retarget those animations to your own skeleton,

196
00:10:24.799 --> 00:10:27.799
you'll find that those animations should work.

197
00:10:27.799 --> 00:10:31.409
Now, most animations on the store

198
00:10:31.409 --> 00:10:33.909
will note whether or not they're compatible

199
00:10:33.909 --> 00:10:35.909
with the Unreal Engine skeleton.

200
00:10:35.909 --> 00:10:37.940
And if that is something you are using,

201
00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:39.440
then a lot of the times,

202
00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:41.440
it will automatically work on that skeleton

203
00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:43.440
without you having to do anything.

204
00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:45.440
But if not, you can use Unreal Engine

205
00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:47.940
retargeting tools, because they're very, very simple,

206
00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:49.940
and as I say, very robust.
